@page "/learn/validation-localization"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Translated validation messages · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Translated validation messages</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your form has to speak German. How many attributes do you have to touch?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> validation looks up every error message in an <code>IStringLocalizer</code>
        (the .NET service that returns text in the user's language). It looks them up by rule name, so one entry
        translates every <code>[Required]</code>. In .NET 10 you had to set up the translation on each attribute.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form in German. <b>Firma</b> (company) is <code>[Required]</code>.
        <b>E-Mail</b> is <code>[Required, EmailAddress]</code>. <b>Firma</b> starts empty and the email starts as
        <code>sales@@</code>, so one press of Save breaks both rules.
    </p>
    <p>
        This version is translated the .NET 10 way: each attribute points at its own translation in a resource
        (a <code>.resx</code> file or a class of strings). One was missed, as often happens in real code. The
        browser's own check is off, so every message you see comes from server validation, the part this page is
        about.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A resource on every attribute, and one missed" Url="demo/validation-localization/suppliers/new/net10?culture=de-DE"
                Label=".NET 10: a resource on every attribute" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Lieferant speichern</b> (Save supplier). <b>Firma</b> says "Firma ist ein Pflichtfeld." in
            German. <b>E-Mail</b> says "The E-Mail field is not a valid e-mail address." in English.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Each attribute translates itself</h2>
    <p>Here's the model behind Fig. 1:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The model the form posts</span>
        <pre class="snip">[Required(ErrorMessageResourceType = typeof(Net10Messages),
          ErrorMessageResourceName = "Required")]          <span class="ok">✓ German</span>
public string? Company { get; set; }

[Required(ErrorMessageResourceType = ..., ErrorMessageResourceName = ...)]
[EmailAddress]                                            <span class="err">✗ English</span>
public string? Email { get; set; }</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 10, Blazor validation never asks an <code>IStringLocalizer</code> for anything. Registering one with
        <code>AddLocalization()</code> doesn't help: plain attributes still answer in English, even on a German
        request. So every attribute has to carry its own translation. Any attribute without one shows its built-in
        English message.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>One localizer, keyed by rule name</h2>
    <p>
        Leave the attributes plain. Put one translation per rule type (<code>Required</code>,
        <code>EmailAddress</code> and so on) in a single shared localizer.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">[ValidatableType]</span>
public sealed class NewSupplierForm
{
    [Required, StringLength(40), Display(Name = "Company")] ...
    [Required, EmailAddress, Display(Name = "Email")] ...
}

<span class="dim">// ValidationMessages.de.resx: {0} is the field's display name</span>
<span class="hl">RequiredAttribute_Error</span>      {0} ist ein Pflichtfeld.
<span class="hl">EmailAddressAttribute_Error</span>  {0} ist keine gültige E-Mail-Adresse.
Company                      Firma

<span class="dim">// Program.cs</span>
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddLocalization();</span>
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddValidation(o =&gt;</span>
<span class="hl">    o.LocalizerProvider = (_, f) =&gt; f.Create(typeof(ValidationMessages)));</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Each key is the attribute's class name plus <code>_Error</code>. The field name that fills <code>{0}</code>
        is translated too: <code>[Display(Name = "Company")]</code> is looked up as <code>Company</code>, so the
        message says "Firma". <code>LocalizerProvider</code> sends every model to the same file. One entry covers
        that rule everywhere in the app, and there's no attribute left to forget.
    </p>
    <p>
        You need both <code>AddValidation()</code> and <code>[ValidatableType]</code>, or the messages stay in
        English. Neither is new: they turn on <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Validation</code>, the validator behind
        <a href="learn/validatable-type-stable">nested validation</a>. Put <code>[ValidatableType]</code> on the
        model the form binds to, and the classes inside it are covered too. What's new in .NET 11 is that this
        validator now asks a localizer for its messages.
    </p>
    <p>Here's the same form, using the plain model.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="Plain attributes, one localizer" Url="demo/validation-localization/suppliers/new?culture=de-DE"
                Label=".NET 11: one localizer">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Lieferant speichern</b>. Both messages are now German: "Firma ist ein Pflichtfeld." and
            "E-Mail ist keine gültige E-Mail-Adresse." None of the attributes has a message setting.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the files behind both demos:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ValidationLocalization/SupplierMessages.cs" Feature="validation-localization">
        <Caption>
            The localizer Fig. 2 uses: one German message per rule, plus the field names for <code>{0}</code>. It's a
            dictionary here so you can see it. A <code>.resx</code> file or a database table works the same way.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ValidationLocalization/ValidationLocalizationSetup.cs" Feature="validation-localization">
        <Caption>
            <code>ValidationOptions.LocalizerProvider</code> chooses the localizer for each model type. This demo only
            sends Fig. 2's model to <code>SupplierMessages</code>, so the rest of this app is unaffected. In your app
            you'd usually send every type to one shared localizer, as in the fix above.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ValidationLocalization/NewSupplierForm.cs" Feature="validation-localization">
        <Caption>
            The model Fig. 2 posts: plain attributes, no message settings. The <code>= "sales@@"</code> on
            <b>Email</b> is just a starting value for the demo. It isn't part of the fix.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ValidationLocalization/NewSupplierFormNet10.cs" Feature="validation-localization">
        <Caption>
            The model Fig. 1 posts: every attribute points at its own translation except <code>[EmailAddress]</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Settings on every attribute</h2>
    <p>In .NET 10 there were three ways to get German messages from a Blazor form. None of them was good:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to translate a message in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[Required(
  <span class="err">ErrorMessageResourceType</span> = typeof(Messages),
  <span class="err">ErrorMessageResourceName</span> = "Required")]</pre>
                <span class="why">point every attribute at its own translation, as Fig. 1 does</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ two settings on every attribute, and any you miss stays English</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">public class LocalizedRequiredAttribute
    : RequiredAttribute { ... }</pre>
                <span class="why">subclass every attribute</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a plain <code>[Required]</code> that slips in stays English, and <code>[EmailAddress]</code> is sealed</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">builder.Services.AddLocalization();
<span class="dim">// "RequiredAttribute_Error" registered</span></pre>
                <span class="why">register a localizer and hope</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ validation never asks it, so messages stay English. Only a name from <code>[Display(ResourceType = ...)]</code> comes out German: "The Firma field is required."</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        .NET 10's <code>ValidationOptions</code> has no <code>LocalizerProvider</code>, so Fig. 4 won't compile
        there. MVC and Razor Pages had <code>AddDataAnnotationsLocalization()</code> for this, but Blazor's
        <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> never used it.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Validation asks the localizer</h2>
    <p>
        Call <code>AddLocalization()</code> and <code>AddValidation()</code>, and mark the model
        <code>[ValidatableType]</code>. Now, when a rule fails, validation looks up its message in an
        <code>IStringLocalizer</code>. It first tries a key that names the model and field, then the model, then just the rule, like
        <code>RequiredAttribute_Error</code>. It
        puts the translated field name in <code>{0}</code>. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you don't set <code>LocalizerProvider</code>, each model gets its own localizer, which reads a
        <code>.resx</code> file named after the model. For <code>MyApp.Forms.NewSupplierForm</code> that's
        <code>Resources/Forms/NewSupplierForm.de.resx</code>, if you called
        <code>AddLocalization(o =&gt; o.ResourcesPath = "Resources")</code>. That works, but you need one file per model, each repeating <code>RequiredAttribute_Error</code>. Set
        <code>LocalizerProvider</code> to share one file instead.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>More specific keys win.</b> <code>NewSupplierForm_Email_EmailAddressAttribute_Error</code> beats <code>NewSupplierForm_EmailAddressAttribute_Error</code>, which beats <code>EmailAddressAttribute_Error</code>.</li>
        <li><b>No key? You get the attribute's own English message.</b> So English needs no entries, and a missing German key falls back to English.</li>
        <li><b>Extra values go in <code>{1}</code>, <code>{2}</code>.</b> <code>StringLength</code> passes its maximum as <code>{1}</code>. Your own attributes can pass values by overriding <code>FormatMessage</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Labels on the page aren't translated by this.</b> Only the <code>{0}</code> in messages uses the validation localizer. The <code>Label</code> and <code>DisplayName</code> components read <code>[Display]</code> directly. Translate labels with <code>[Display(ResourceType = …)]</code>, or by hand as these demos do.</li>
        <li><b>The browser check uses the same translations.</b> On a static form with <a href="learn/ssr-client-validation">client-side validation</a> turned on, the server writes the translated messages into the page. These demos turn the browser check off so you see the server's messages.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
